WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:02.741
A plant is building a new hall.

2
00:00:03.423 --> 00:00:07.985
Packing line 3 has to move there, during the summer shutdown of 3 weeks.

3
00:00:08.697 --> 00:00:11.658
The sponsor comes with a request, move line 3.

4
00:00:12.618 --> 00:00:14.882
But a request is not a project yet.

5
00:00:15.636 --> 00:00:20.106
First comes the intake, from the client’s question to a project worth doing.

6
00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:24.710
We follow this one request through the four topics of the module.

7
00:00:25.444 --> 00:00:27.929
The details come later, in the lessons.

8
00:00:29.263 --> 00:00:31.690
The first topic is From question to problem.

9
00:00:32.402 --> 00:00:35.780
Move line 3 is a solution, not a problem yet.

10
00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:39.361
A project aimed at it may fix the wrong thing.

11
00:00:40.397 --> 00:00:42.429
Ask about the problem underneath.

12
00:00:43.159 --> 00:00:45.481
Why does it have to happen, and when is it done?

13
00:00:46.509 --> 00:00:49.795
The answer is the goal, what must be measurably different.

14
00:00:50.569 --> 00:00:55.259
After the shutdown line 3 runs in hall B, as fast as it does now.

15
00:00:56.298 --> 00:01:00.652
To dig into the problem we often use the 5 Whys and an issue tree.

16
00:01:01.943 --> 00:01:04.637
The second topic is Stakeholders and trust.

17
00:01:05.403 --> 00:01:07.539
Who decides, and who can stop it?

18
00:01:08.577 --> 00:01:13.035
A stakeholder is anyone who affects the project or is affected by it.

19
00:01:14.051 --> 00:01:17.627
The sponsor owns the budget and the result, and decides.

20
00:01:18.348 --> 00:01:22.075
The process owner, here the team lead, has to live with the change.

21
00:01:23.144 --> 00:01:26.557
The operators are the users, because their work changes.

22
00:01:27.298 --> 00:01:30.432
And a blocker can stop it, even without saying no.

23
00:01:31.138 --> 00:01:35.411
Here that is the works council, if operators have to work in the shutdown.

24
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:40.225
For stakeholders we often use a stakeholder map and a kick-off.

25
00:01:41.515 --> 00:01:43.767
The third topic is Scope and charter.

26
00:01:44.525 --> 00:01:45.326
When is it done?

27
00:01:46.327 --> 00:01:48.359
Write the goal in one sentence.

28
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.364
And take a baseline.

29
00:01:51.061 --> 00:01:53.104
How fast does line 3 run now?

30
00:01:53.793 --> 00:01:55.860
Without it nobody can say it worked.

31
00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.317
Then the scope, what the project covers.

32
00:02:00.092 --> 00:02:01.381
Line 3 is in.

33
00:02:02.136 --> 00:02:05.387
Line 4 stays where it is, so it is out of scope.

34
00:02:06.402 --> 00:02:09.862
For the scope we often use a project charter and a SIPOC.

35
00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:14.110
The fourth topic is Business case and proposal.

36
00:02:14.807 --> 00:02:15.887
Is it worth doing?

37
00:02:16.896 --> 00:02:20.042
A business case shows whether the project is worth it.

38
00:02:20.768 --> 00:02:24.099
What does it cost, what does it yield, and how certain is that?

39
00:02:25.162 --> 00:02:29.470
One-off costs you pay once, like the installer and the new switchboard.

40
00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:34.103
The benefits are the measurable gain, preferably in euros.

41
00:02:34.815 --> 00:02:36.998
Here the rent of the old hall stops.

42
00:02:38.098 --> 00:02:41.639
Then comes the proposal, with approach, result and price.

43
00:02:42.343 --> 00:02:44.375
The sponsor says yes or no to it.

44
00:02:45.439 --> 00:02:48.040
For the business case and the proposal we often

45
00:02:48.109 --> 00:02:51.523
use the payback period and an SCQ as the opening.

46
00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:55.868
Together they give the sponsor what is needed to decide.

47
00:02:56.602 --> 00:02:59.435
The goal, the people, the scope and what it yields.

48
00:03:00.443 --> 00:03:01.558
It all connects.

49
00:03:02.256 --> 00:03:04.578
Without a goal you cannot test the scope.

50
00:03:05.344 --> 00:03:07.968
If the sponsor says yes, planning begins.

51
00:03:09.303 --> 00:03:10.975
This is the map of Projects.

52
00:03:11.687 --> 00:03:15.182
The first station on the road of a project is the intake.

53
00:03:16.216 --> 00:03:19.491
The intake has four topics, each with four levels.

54
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.445
Start at the top, with From question to problem, at the first level.

55
00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:30.091
At the second level you hold the first conversation, build trust in the first weeks,

56
00:03:30.196 --> 00:03:33.377
put the assignment on one page and write the proposal.

57
00:03:34.377 --> 00:03:37.152
At the third level you sharpen the problem statement,

58
00:03:37.372 --> 00:03:40.391
make agreements with the sponsor and draw boundaries.

59
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.372
At the fourth you work with hypotheses,

60
00:03:43.582 --> 00:03:46.786
build a coalition and split the project into phases.

61
00:03:47.798 --> 00:03:51.943
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
